      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "f076792b-9d21-586f-91a7-608309a4888d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Borodin, Ian Sexton\n- Works: Stranger in Paradise, Polovtsian Dances, Prince Igor, Gliding Dance of the Maidens, Messa da Requiem, Requiem (Opus 9)\n- Organizations: Cór Cois Abhann\n- Groups: Kipchaks, Cumans, Cumans-Kipchaks, Rus' people\n- Places: Georgia, Central Asia, Eastern Europe\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Borodin composed the opera Prince Igor, which premiered in 1890.\n- The Polovtsian Dances form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera Prince Igor.\n- The Gliding Dance of the Maidens is part of the Polovtsian Dances in Alexander Borodin's opera Prince Igor.\n- The song Stranger in Paradise uses a melody taken from Alexander Borodin's Gliding Dance of the Maidens from the Polovtsian Dances.\n- Polovtsian Dances can also be spelled Polovetsian Dances.\n- The Russian title Половецкие пляски is transliterated as Polovetskie plyaski.\n- The name Polovtsy was used by the Rus' people for the Kipchaks and Cumans.\n- The Cumans-Kipchaks in Georgia were an ancient nomadic Turkic people who occupied territories from Central Asia to Eastern Europe.\n- Cór Cois Abhann is currently directed by Ian Sexton.\n- Cór Cois Abhann has performed large-scale works including Verdi's Messa da Requiem, Borodin's Polovtsian Dances, and Duruflé's Requiem (Opus 9).",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "6774d404-8e87-54df-afc9-775f9aff1110",
        "text": "Page: Stranger in Paradise (song). Sentence: Like almost all the music in that show, the melody was taken from music composed by Alexander Borodin (1833–1887), in this case, the \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens\", from the \"Polovtsian Dances\" in the opera \"Prince Igor\" (1890).\nPage: Polovtsian Dances. Sentence: The Polovtsian Dances, or Polovetsian Dances (Russian: Половецкие пляски, \"Polovetskie plyaski\" from the Russian \"Polovtsy\"—the name given to the Kipchaks and Cumans by the Rus' people) form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera \"Prince Igor\".\nPage: Cór Cois Abhann. Sentence: The choir is currently directed by Ian Sexton and has performed large scale works including Verdi's \"Messa da Requiem, Borodin's \"Polovtsian Dances\" and Duruflé's \"Requiem (Opus 9)\".\nPage: Kipchaks in Georgia. Sentence: The Cumans-Kipchaks in Georgia are of an ancient nomadic Turkic people who occupied large territories from Central Asia to Eastern Europe.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
